<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Ideal or real—that is the question. In this work, we explore whether principles from game theory can be effectively applied to the evaluation of large language models (LLMs). This inquiry is motivated by the growing inadequacy of conventional evaluation practices, which often rely on fixed-format tasks with reference answers and struggle to capture the nuanced, subjective, and open-ended nature of modern LLM behavior.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose a novel alternative: automatic mutual evaluation, where LLMs assess each other’s output through self-play and peer review. These peer assessments are then systematically compared with human voting behavior to evaluate their alignment with human judgment. Our framework incorporates game-theoretic voting algorithms to aggregate peer reviews, enabling a principled investigation into whether model-generated rankings reflect human preferences. Empirical results reveal both convergences and divergences between theoretical predictions and human evaluations, offering valuable insights into the promises and limitations of mutual evaluation. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to jointly integrate mutual evaluation, game-theoretic aggregation, and human-grounded validation for evaluating the capabilities of LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measuring the performance of large language models (LLMs) remains a challenging task due to their broad and diverse capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al. (2024)</a></cite>. To complement traditional evaluation methods, we focus on a game-theoretic perspective. <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Game theory</span>, as a theoretical framework for analyzing strategy selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hansen et al. (2025)</a></cite> and utility distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirfakhar et al. (2025)</a></cite> among interacting agents, emphasizes mechanisms of competition, cooperation, and dynamic feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fabbri et al. (2021)</a></cite>. These properties naturally align with the inherent capability differences, judgment conflicts, and task adaptability among LLMs, enabling game-theoretic approaches to effectively simulate complex human evaluation processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pasch (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Conversely, LLMs offer an ideal testbed for empirically validating theoretical insights from game theory. Motivated by these observations, we ask: <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can game-theoretic methodologies yield model rankings that align with human judgments in evaluating the capabilities of LLMs?</span> Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an illustrative overview of this central question.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15746v1/game-theory-for-llm-evaluation.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/149;" width="191" height="149" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Illustration of game-theoretic peer evaluation for LLM performance ranking.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the aforementioned hypothesis, one key challenge lies in the presence of self-preference bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dietz et al. (2025)</a></cite>, where models tend to favor their own outputs when serving as evaluators. Such bias can compromise the fairness and objectivity of peer assessment. To address this, we adopt an automated peer evaluation mechanism utilizing large models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> to simulate a game-theoretic assessment process. Specifically, we formalize this process as a game-theoretic voting system and introduce the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decentralized peer ranking</span> framework, where each LLM simultaneously serves as both an evaluatee and an evaluator without relying on a central judge. It generates pairwise preferences over the outputs of its peers, which are then aggregated into a global preference matrix that captures collective judgments across models. Based on this matrix, we apply a suite of aggregation algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Charikar et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Charikar et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zheng (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wood et al. (2024)</a></cite> to derive model rankings and systematically compare voting rules in terms of their alignment with human evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This decentralized peer ranking framework offers a novel approach to LLM evaluation by leveraging model-to-model interaction for collaborative assessment. By treating each model as both an evaluatee and an evaluator, the framework enables more informative pairwise comparisons and captures nuanced judgments that may be overlooked in traditional evaluation setups. However, the effectiveness and fairness of such a system depend on several critical factors. Before presenting the experimental design, it is important to clarify the core research objectives our study seeks to address:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">During the peer evaluation, how well do different game-theoretic peer evaluation algorithms align with human judgments in ranking LLMs?</span> This question probes the core hypothesis of our work. We aim to determine if the collective intelligence derived from LLM peer evaluation can reflect human preference of performance, and to identify which aggregation algorithms most effectively translate pairwise comparisons into reliable global rankings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can game-theoretic ranking algorithms mitigate such self-bias and produce more reliable evaluation rankings?</span> Prior studies have demonstrated that LLMs often exhibit self-preference bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dietz et al. (2025)</a></cite>, where models tend to assign favorable scores to their own outputs when serving as evaluators. This phenomenon poses a challenge to the fairness and objectivity of model evaluation. We investigate whether game-theoretic methods, which aggregate rankings from multiple evaluators based on collective decision-making ranking, can effectively reduce this bias and yield more trustworthy assessment results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Are there specific LLM capabilities where game-theoretic evaluation exhibits varying alignment with human judgment?</span> LLMs possess diverse capabilities across a wide range of tasks, from logical reasoning to creative generation. Analyzing alignment across different task categories helps identify where this evaluation method performs reliably, and where it may fall short in capturing human preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical analysis is organized into three components to systematically evaluate the effectiveness and fairness of game-theoretic peer evaluation for LLMs. First, we compare model rankings produced by different aggregation algorithms against human-annotated ground truth to assess alignment with human judgment. Second, we quantify self-preference bias by measuring the extent to which LLMs favor their own outputs, and examine whether rank aggregation mitigates this bias through collective decision-making. Third, we analyze alignment scores across different task types (e.g., mathematic, chinese, instruction-following, open-ended generation and so on) to investigate whether evaluation consistency varies by capability domain. Together, these experiments provide a comprehensive assessment of our framework and highlight its potential for reliable, and fair model evaluation. In summary, this paper makes the following key contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate peer evaluation among LLMs as a game-theoretic voting problem, establishing a principled framework for systematically comparing model capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that robust aggregation algorithms can yield model rankings that closely align with human judgments, enabling fair, scalable, and trustworthy evaluation of LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically identify systematic self-assessment biases in LLMs and propose practical strategies to mitigate them within evaluation protocols.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15746v1/fig2-framework.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/303;" width="381" height="303" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The proposed framework for game-theoretic evaluation of LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Evaluation Methods for LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating LLMs remains a central yet challenging problem due to their rapidly expanding capabilities. Existing methods can be broadly categorized into two paradigms: <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">human-annotated evaluation</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-a-judge evaluation</span>. Traditional benchmarks such as MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite> and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite> rely on curated datasets with reference answers and human or rule-based grading schemes. These approaches offer consistency and reproducibility, enabling fair model comparisons. However, they face increasing concerns about overfitting to static test sets, which may compromise the generalizability of evaluation results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An increasingly popular alternative is to use LLMs themselves as evaluators. This <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-a-judge</span> paradigm enables scalable and flexible evaluation, particularly suited to open-ended or subjective tasks where reference answers are lacking. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al. (2024a)</a></cite> propose selecting a single LLM as the evaluator through a qualification stage, after which it is used to evaluate all candidate responses. This approach can introduce evaluator bias and limited perspective, especially if the selected model shares architectural or behavioral similarities with the models being judged. In contrast, our method adopts a fully decentralized evaluation paradigm: rather than relying on a single judge, we incorporate feedback from all participating models and apply game-theoretic aggregation to derive a consensus ranking. This approach mitigates individual bias and provides a more diverse and representative assessment of model performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Game-Theoretic Approaches to Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Game-theoretic methods provide a general framework for evaluation based on relative comparisons and strategic interactions, without relying on fixed ground-truth labels. Prior work in this area has explored two main directions. The first focuses on constructing game-inspired environments to examine decision-making and strategic behavior, particularly in the context of language models. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akata et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023)</a></cite> develop benchmarks based on matrix games and auctions to study how models behave under varying payoff structures. While such setups are valuable for behavioral analysis, they are primarily used for probing rather than for evaluating performance or generating rankings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second direction leverages preference aggregation techniques from social choice theory to derive consensus rankings from pairwise comparisons. These methods help reduce evaluator bias and increase robustness when scoring outputs that may be noisy or subjective. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose an enhanced Borda count (EBC) algorithm to re-rank math reasoning responses based on comparative judgments. Such aggregation strategies have been applied across domains including LLM evaluation, recommender systems, and tournament design, where they facilitate consistent evaluation in the absence of absolute ground truth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview of Our Evaluation Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional evaluation methods for LLMs primarily rely on comparisons against predefined reference answers. However, this approach risks overfitting, where models exploit surface patterns in benchmarks rather than demonstrating robust reasoning or generalization capabilities. Such behavior undermines both the fairness and validity of evaluation. Furthermore, human-annotated evaluations remain costly, subjective, and inherently limited in scalability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we propose an automated peer evaluation framework in which LLMs assess the outputs of all models, including their own. Each model serves as both an evaluatee and an evaluator, producing rankings over peer responses. These decentralized judgments are aggregated via game-theoretic voting algorithms to produce a coherent global model ranking, enabling analysis of alignment with human preferences. The overall framework consists of three core components: peer evaluation protocol, aggregation over per-question rankings, and alignment with human judgment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Peer Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{LLM_{1},\dots,LLM_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{LLM_{1},\dots,LLM_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a set of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> language models, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},\dots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},\dots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represent a shared evaluation question set. Each model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LLM_{i}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LLM_{i}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a bundled response <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathcal{Q}}=\{a_{i1},\dots,a_{in}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathcal{Q}}=\{a_{i1},\dots,a_{in}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-th model’s answer to question <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in the upper part of Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the evaluation process comprises two stages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response Generation.</span> Each model independently answers every question <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing a response <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LLM_{i}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LLM_{i}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decentralized Peer Evaluation.</span> For each question <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, every model <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LLM_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LLM_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as an evaluator and is presented with the anonymized responses <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{1j},a_{2j},\dots,a_{mj}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{1j},a_{2j},\dots,a_{mj}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, including its own. The evaluator provides a complete ranking over the responses based on their quality with respect to the given question.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Each of these rankings is converted into a set of pairwise preferences, such as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{xj}\succ a_{yj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{xj}\succ a_{yj}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the evaluator prefers model <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>’s response over that of model <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for question <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Aggregating these preferences across evaluators and questions results in a preference structure that forms the basis for downstream model ranking. This evaluation framework facilitates automated, model-driven assessment and also enables the study of phenomena such as self-preference bias, which we analyze in subsequent sections.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Aggregation over Per-Question Rankings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the collection of pairwise preferences obtained from peer evaluations, we aim to derive a global ranking over models that reflects their overall performance. This is formulated as a rank aggregation problem, where the goal is to combine individual rankings, each derived from a specific evaluation question, into a consensus ranking that best captures the overall preference structure. Since input rankings may vary due to evaluator differences or local noise, the aggregation method must be both robust and consistent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in our experiments, the Kemeny-Young algorithm consistently achieves the highest alignment with human preferences, and is therefore adopted as the default aggregation strategy. This method identifies the ranking that maximizes overall alignment with the input rankings by minimizing pairwise discordance. Its objective is to find the ranking that has the smallest total disagreement, measured by Kendall-Tau distance, with all input rankings. Alternative aggregation strategies, including Borda count and Copeland voting, are discussed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Rerank Methods ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, along with implementation details and comparative analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Alignment with Human Judgment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the validity of our framework, we compare the rankings produced by our game-theoretic evaluation method against human preference ranking. Specifically, we align our rankings with those derived from the Chatbot Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a crowdsourced human evaluation platform introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, where annotators vote between model outputs in pairwise comparisons. The aggregated results from this arena provide a widely accepted benchmark of human preferences over LLMs. We compute several alignment metrics to quantify the similarity between our game-theoretic rankings and those from Chatbot Arena:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pearson correlation coefficient</span>: Measures the linear correlation between model scores in the two rankings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kendall’s <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall (1948)</a></cite>: Measures rank correlation between two orderings.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">High alignment scores indicate that decentralized model-driven evaluation can effectively approximate aggregated human judgments, offering a scalable and label-free alternative to traditional human evaluation pipelines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:97.7pt;vertical-align:-47.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-148.7pt,42.1pt) scale(0.537115718296941,0.537115718296941) ;">
