<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution of Pearson and Kendall correlation coefficients</span> between model-generated rankings and human preferences on the GSM8K dataset. Each value reflects <span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">micro-level alignment</span>, computed as the correlation between a model-generated ranking and the corresponding human ranking per question. The leftmost columns show the distribution of these correlation scores for individual LLMs, while the remaining columns report the same statistics after applying different aggregation algorithms. Reported metrics include the mean, standard deviation (Std.), minimum (Min), 25th, 50th (median), 75th percentiles, and maximum (Max) values. Higher values indicate stronger alignment with human judgment. Color intensity reflects the magnitude of alignment, with darker orange indicating higher correlation. Abbreviations: Avg = average voting, Dod = Dodgson, Cop = Copeland, Bor = Borda, Irv = instant-runoff voting, Spm = Spearman, Kem = Kemeny-Young, Ken = Kendall. Models: 4o11 = GPT-4o-2024-11-20, 4o05 = GPT-4o-2024-05-13, 4o08 = GPT-4o-2024-08-06, Sn10 = Claude-3.5-Sonnet-20241022, Hk10 = Claude-3.5-Haiku-20241022, Op02 = Claude-3-Opus-20240229.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setups</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate model reasoning across a diverse set of benchmarks, including GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite>, GPQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al. (2023)</a></cite>, CEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, IFEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and the Creative Writing benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. All datasets are subsampled for tractability while preserving diversity across domains and reasoning types. Further details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Dataset Statistics and Selection Criteria ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments include six recent LLMs from OpenAI (GPT-4o variants) and Anthropic (Claude-3 and 3.5 series), chosen for their top-tier performance in recent benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human Preference Reference.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate how well our framework aligns with human preferences, we compare the resulting model rankings against those from Chatbot Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Since Chatbot Arena provides system-level rankings across multiple domains, we match each benchmark to the most relevant leaderboard subset. Specifically, we use the GSM8K for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">math-specific</span> ranking, the MMLU and GPQA for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overall</span> ranking, the CEval for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chinese</span> ranking, the IFEval for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instruction-following</span> ranking, and the MBPP for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">code-related</span> rankings. This task-to-domain mapping ensures that our comparisons reflect the most appropriate human preference signals. Alignment is quantified using both Pearson and Kendall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall (1948)</a></cite> correlation coefficients between our aggregated rankings and those from Chatbot Arena.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Levels.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess how well model-generated rankings align with human preferences, we evaluate correlation at two complementary levels: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">micro-level</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">macro-level</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Micro-level correlation</span> evaluates alignment at the level of individual questions. For each question in a dataset, we compute the correlation between the rankings from a specific aggregation method and the corresponding human reference (e.g., from Chatbot Arena), yielding a sequence of correlation scores. To assess both the consistency and quality of this alignment, we analyze their distribution using descriptive statistics such as the mean, standard deviation, and key percentiles. For instance, a higher 25th percentile indicates that at least three-quarters of the evaluation questions yield rankings with strong alignment to human judgments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Macro-level correlation</span> computes the average of per-question rankings to obtain a single overall ranking of models for the entire dataset, and then computes a single correlation score with the human-provided global ranking. This captures overall alignment at the dataset level but does not reflect per-question variability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ranking Algorithms.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To produce a final ranking over models from decentralized peer evaluations, we implement nine classical aggregation methods from social choice theory and voting literature: Average, Borda Count <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saari (1985)</a></cite>, Copeland <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saari and Merlin (1996)</a></cite>, Dodgson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brandt (2009)</a></cite>, Instant-Runoff Voting (IRV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brandt (2009)</a></cite>, Kemeny-Young <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kemeny (1959)</a></cite>, Kendall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall (1948)</a></cite>, and Spearman. These algorithms take as input either ranking lists or pairwise preferences collected across evaluation questions and produce an overall consensus ranking of models. They cover a broad range of principles, including positional scoring methods such as Borda, pairwise comparison approaches such as Copeland, and consistency-based objectives such as Kemeny. Further implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Rerank Methods ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the alignment between game-theoretic methods and human judgment, we compare model rankings produced by various aggregation algorithms against the human-established leaderboard from Chatbot Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. We report both Pearson and Kendall correlation coefficients to quantify the alignment. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Alignment with Human Judgment ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the correlation distributions on the GSM8K dataset, while additional results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Supplementary experiment on Q1 ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> due to space constraints.</p>
</div>
