<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment Between Macro-Level Model Rankings and Human Preferences.</span> <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc</span> denotes the <span id="S4.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">correlation score</span> between human rankings and the model rankings induced by overall accuracy. <span id="S4.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kem</span> denotes the <span id="S4.T2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">correlation score</span> between human rankings and the model rankings derived from Kemeny-young aggregation over per-question peer evaluations. Pearson (Prs) and Kendall (Ken) coefficients are reported, with higher values indicating stronger alignment with human judgments from Chatbot Arena.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Re-ranking Methods Achieve Higher Alignment with Human Judgment than Single-Model Evaluations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Alignment with Human Judgment ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, all rank aggregation algorithms, with the exception of IRV, consistently achieve stronger alignment with human judgments compared to most individual models. This improvement is reflected not only in higher average correlation scores, but also in lower variance and more stable distributions. For example, both <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kendall</span> reach a median Pearson correlation of 0.771, surpassing the best individual model, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-2024-11-20</span>, which achieves a lower median of 0.714 and a wider interquartile range. The higher 50th percentile indicates that in over half of the evaluation questions, aggregation methods yield correlation scores exceeding 0.77, reflecting strong alignment with human preferences. Overall, this demonstrates that game-theoretic aggregation improves not only average alignment, but also consistency across evaluations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the aggregation side, the Kemeny-Young algorithm demonstrates strong performance, achieving the highest median correlations with human preferences (both 0.771) along with narrow interquartile ranges. This indicates not only strong average alignment, but also low variance across evaluation instances. In contrast, methods such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRV</span> exhibit lower mean correlations and wider distribution spreads, suggesting greater inconsistency in capturing human-aligned rankings. While <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kendall</span> achieves comparable performance to <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny-Young</span> on GSM8K (as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Alignment with Human Judgment ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the results in Table <a href="#A6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix F Supplementary experiment on Q1 ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reveal that <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny-Young</span> consistently outperforms <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kendall</span> across other benchmarks, highlighting its superior generalizability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While overall accuracy is commonly used to summarize a model’s performance on a dataset, it remains unclear how well this metric aligns with human preferences. To address this, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">macro-level correlation</span>, which measures the correlation between model rankings and human judgments at the dataset level. Specifically, we compare the correlation between human rankings and two types of model rankings: one based on overall accuracy across the dataset, and another derived from game-theoretic aggregation of per-question peer evaluations. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we apply the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny-Young</span> algorithm to obtain per-question rankings, then compute their average to generate a macro-level ranking. Correlation scores are then computed between this aggregated ranking and human preferences. Across all benchmarks, this method consistently achieves higher alignment with human judgments than accuracy-based rankings. Furthermore, in the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Creative Writing</span> dataset, which lacks definitive reference answers, game-theoretic aggregation still maintains strong correlation with human assessments, underscoring its robustness in subjective evaluation settings. These results suggest that collective preference–based aggregation provides a more faithful approximation of human evaluation than conventional accuracy metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Besides, among individual models, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-2024-11-20</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4o11</span>) stands out with the highest median correlation (0.714) and the smallest standard deviation (0.222), reflecting not only strong alignment with human rankings but also consistent performance across diverse evaluation questions. In contrast, models like Claude-3.5-Sonnet (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sn10</span>) and Claude-3.5-Haiku (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Hk10</span>) show larger variability and notably lower average correlations, indicating less reliability in mirroring human judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these findings reinforce the effectiveness of game-theoretic rank aggregation. They highlight the potential of such methods not only to outperform individual LLMs in aligning with human judgment but also to do so with improved consistency and robustness at both the micro (per-question) and macro (overall ranking) levels.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.15746v1/self-others.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/228;" width="215" height="228" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment with Human Judgments under Different Evaluation Protocols.</span>
This figure reports the distribution of Pearson correlation coefficients between model-generated rankings and human preferences from Chatbot Arena under four evaluation protocols: SE, PE, SIE, and SFE. In each boxplot, a higher box indicates stronger alignment with human rankings, while a shorter box implies lower variance and thus more stable alignment. Additional results are provided in the appendix due to space constraints.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><sup id="S4.F3.4.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> Further experimental details are provided in Appendix X.</p></div></div>
</figure>
