<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Rankings under Different Evaluation Protocols.</span> This table presents model rankings on three representative benchmarks under four evaluation protocols: SE, PE, SIE, and SFE, where lower values indicate better rankings (1 = best, 6 = worst). Comparing these values allows us to assess the impact of self-preference and the robustness of aggregation. For rows corresponding to PE, the values in parentheses indicate the change in ranking relative to SE. For SFE, the parentheses indicate the change in ranking relative to SIE. Models: 4o-1120 = GPT-4o-2024-11-20, 4o-0513 = GPT-4o-2024-05-13, 4o-0806 = GPT-4o-2024-08-06, Sn-1022 = Claude-3.5-Sonnet-20241022, Hk-1022 = Claude-3.5-Haiku-20241022, Op-0229 = Claude-3-Opus-20240229. Due to space constraints, results on additional benchmarks are provided in the appendix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15746v1/task-eval.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/228;" width="215" height="228" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment with Human Judgments Across Benchmarks.</span> Each boxplot shows the distribution of Pearson correlations between human rankings and rankings aggregated by the Kemeny-Young method under the <span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">micro-level</span> setting. Higher values indicate stronger alignment. Purple dots represent <span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">macro-level</span> correlations for each benchmark. “CreWrite” denotes the creative writing benchmark, and “InstFol” refers to instruction following.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Q2: Can Game-Theoretic Re-ranking Mitigate Self-Preference Bias in LLMs?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research has demonstrated that LLMs commonly exhibit <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-preference bias</span> when evaluating their own outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dietz et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In such cases, models tend to assign favorable scores to their own generations, compromising the objectivity of evaluation outcomes. This phenomenon raises concerns about the reliability of LLMs as autonomous evaluators, particularly in decentralized or multi-agent settings where self-assessment can distort collective judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate this issue, we examine whether game-theoretic re-ranking, specifically consensus aggregation using the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny-Young</span> method, can mitigate self-preference bias and lead to more objective model evaluations. We define four evaluation protocols to isolate the effects of self-assessment and peer assessment. Self Evaluation <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(SE)</span> collects the rankings generated by each model when serving as an evaluator, including the position it assigns to itself. These self-generated rankings are then used to construct an overall model ranking, allowing us to observe the extent of self-preference bias. Peer Evaluation <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(PE)</span> considers only the rankings provided by other models, excluding all self-evaluations, and averages the rankings each model receives from its peers. Self-Inclusive Evaluation <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(SIE)</span> aggregates all rankings, including both peer and self-evaluations, using the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kemeny-Young</span> method to compute a consensus ranking. In contrast, Self-Free Evaluation <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(SFE)</span> applies the same aggregation procedure but excludes all self-generated rankings, enabling a clearer view of peer-only assessments and providing a cleaner estimate of bias introduced by self-evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Game-Theoretic Aggregation Mitigates Self-Preference Bias.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy. ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, LLMs exhibit a clear <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-preference bias</span>. Across all benchmarks, SE consistently yields more favorable self-assigned rankings compared to peer evaluations in PE. For instance, on GSM8K, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4o-1120</span> improves by +0.44 positions when evaluated by others (from 2.00 in SE to 2.44 in PE), and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Hk-1022</span> shows an even greater shift of +0.49 (3.86 to 4.35). These results confirm that models tend to overrate their own performance. Notably, on GSM8K, MMLU, and GPQA, more than three models demonstrate inflated self-rankings under SE compared to PE. Even more strikingly, as shown in Table <a href="#A7.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix G Supplementary experiment on Q2 ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, five models exhibit self-preference in the instruction-following (IFEval) benchmark. To assess whether game-theoretic aggregation can mitigate this bias, we compare rankings from SIE and SFE using the Kemeny-Young method. While SE–PE differences are often substantial, SIE-SFE differences are generally modest. For example, on GSM8K, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4o-0513</span> shifts by only -0.02 (from 3.08 in SIE to 3.06 in SFE), and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4o-1120</span> shifts by +0.18. These small deltas indicate that aggregation substantially reduces the influence of self-preference. Overall, the SIE and SFE rankings are much closer than those from SE and PE, suggesting that the aggregation procedure helps recover more balanced and objective rankings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate whether these rankings align with human judgment, Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy. ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the distribution of Pearson correlations between model-generated rankings and human preferences across evaluation questions. The results show that rankings from SE yield the weakest and most variable alignment with human judgments, indicating that models’ self-assigned rankings are often unreliable. In contrast, SIE achieves the strongest alignment overall, suggesting that incorporating all available rankings, including self-evaluations, can produce results that are most consistent with human preferences. Furthermore, the correlation distributions of SIE and SFE are close, indicating that game-theoretic aggregation is effective in mitigating self-preference bias, even when self-evaluations are included. Due to space constraints, we report results on three representative benchmarks in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy. ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy. ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with full results provided in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Supplementary experiment on Q2 ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Q3: Which Abilities of LLMs Can Be Reliably Evaluated Through Game Theory?</h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Game-Theoretic Evaluation Yields the Most Human-Aligned Rankings in Math Tasks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Kemeny Aggregation Emerges as the Most Effective Re-ranking Strategy. ‣ 4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows how well game-theoretic aggregation using the Kemeny-Young algorithm aligns with human preferences across different types of LLM capabilities. For each benchmark, we report both micro-level and macro-level Pearson correlations with human rankings from the Chatbot Arena. The box plots display the distribution of micro-level correlations across individual questions, while the purple dots represent macro-level correlations aggregated over the dataset. Among all tasks, the GSM8K benchmark shows the strongest alignment with human judgment. Game-theoretic aggregation achieves both the highest median micro-level correlation and the smallest interquartile range, indicating stable and consistent alignment across questions. At the macro level, it reaches a Pearson correlation of 0.941, outperforming all other datasets. In contrast, benchmarks involving CEval or Creative Writing exhibit lower and more variable correlations, suggesting that capturing human preferences in linguistically nuanced tasks remains more challenging. These findings suggest that game-theoretic aggregation is particularly effective in tasks with clearer evaluation criteria, such as math and code generation. While it also shows promise in more subjective tasks, additional factors such as task context or stylistic variation may need to be considered to better align with human judgment. Notably, despite high micro-level variance, the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Creative Writing</span> benchmark achieves remarkably high macro-level alignment with human preferences (0.914), indicating that aggregate judgments can still reliably reflect human consensus. Overall, the results highlight the applicability of peer-based evaluation across diverse capabilities while also revealing its current limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore the potential of game-theoretic methodologies for evaluating the performance of artificial intelligence agents in the context of LLMs. By incorporating peer assessments, our proposed framework demonstrates close alignment with human judgments. Notably, game-theoretic aggregation also mitigates self-preference bias, leading to fairer and more objective evaluations. These findings underscore the promise of game-theoretic evaluation as a scalable alternative to traditional manual benchmarks, while also revealing key limitations and directions for future research. For instance, the effectiveness of our approach varies across task types, achieving stronger alignment in objective domains than in creative writing. We anticipate broader applications and empirical validations at the intersection of game theory and LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
