<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="A1.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution of Pearson correlation coefficients</span> between model-generated rankings and human preferences on the <span id="A1.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenOverall</span>, <span id="A1.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenMath</span> and <span id="A1.T4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenChinese</span> dataset. Each value reflects <span id="A1.T4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">micro-level alignment</span>, computed as the correlation between a model-generated ranking and the corresponding human ranking per question. The leftmost columns show the distribution of these correlation scores for individual LLMs, while the remaining columns report the same statistics after applying different aggregation algorithms. Reported metrics include the mean, standard deviation (Std.), minimum (Min), 25th, 50th (median), 75th percentiles, and maximum (Max) values. Higher values indicate stronger alignment with human judgment. Color intensity reflects the magnitude of alignment, with darker orange indicating higher correlation. Abbreviations: Avg = average voting, Dod = Dodgson, Cop = Copeland, Bor = Borda, Irv = instant-runoff voting, Spm = Spearman, Kem = Kemeny-Young, Ken = Kendall. Models: 4o11 = GPT-4o-2024-11-20, 4o05 = GPT-4o-2024-05-13, 4o08 = GPT-4o-2024-08-06, Sn10 = Claude-3.5-Sonnet-20241022, Hk10 = Claude-3.5-Haiku-20241022, Op02 = Claude-3-Opus-20240229.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional evaluation of LLMs often depends on <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static datasets</span>, which are fixed sets of benchmark questions accompanied by reference answers, such as MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite> and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. While these datasets consisten consistent and reproducible comparisons, they suffer from inherent limitations. In particular, many static benchmarks have become publicly available and widely used, increasing the risk of data leakage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023b)</a></cite> and benchmark saturation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, leading to inflated performance estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our game-theoretic evaluation framework is naturally suited for evaluating model performance on datasets beyond traditional benchmarks, as it depends solely on relative peer assessments rather than predefined ground-truth annotations. To demonstrate this capability, we construct a suite of novel evaluation sets using the latest version of GPT-4. These datasets, including <span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenMath</span>, <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenChinese</span> and <span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GenOverall</span>, are designed to assess three core capabilities of LLMs: mathematical reasoning, chinese and general comprehension. All questions are newly generated and not seen during pretraining, ensuring evaluation under distributional shift. Prompt templates and generation details are provided in the section <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Generation Prompts for Capability-Specific Benchmarks ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.
Aggregated via game-theoretic voting, these peer evaluations produce global model rankings, which we compare against human preferences. This methodology facilitates a more rigorous examination of generalization in out-of-distribution settings and extends the applicability of our framework beyond the constraints of static benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix A Robust Human Alignment via Synthetic Evaluation ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, despite being constructed synthetically, the generated datasets yield alignment results highly consistent with those observed in Experiment <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Q1: Can Game-Theoretic Evaluation Align with Human Judgment? ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> <span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>. Our game-theoretic evaluation framework continues to produce rankings that strongly correlate with human preferences across both Pearson and Kendall metrics. Notably, methods such as Kemeny-Young (<span id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kem</span>) again demonstrate the highest median and upper-quartile correlations, reaffirming their robustness. These findings demonstrate that our peer-based aggregation approach is robust to potential data leakage in the benchmark. Furthermore, it maintains strong alignment with human preferences even on entirely novel, LLM-generated datasets, highlighting its reliability across a wide range of evaluation conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Effect of Truncated Rankings in Peer Evaluation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural extension of our framework involves permitting <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">truncated or partially ordered rankings</span>, in which an LLM ranks only its top-<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> preferred responses while treating all remaining candidates as <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tied for the lowest position</span>. This relaxation reduces the burden on evaluators and can enhance scalability when the number of candidate responses is large. To examine the practical implications of this setting, we conducted supplementary experiments on the GSM8K dataset. Each evaluator was asked to produce rankings under two conditions. In the <span id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complete linear ranking</span> condition, evaluators provided a full ordering over all candidate responses. In the <span id="A2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">truncated ranking</span> condition, evaluators reported only their top three preferred responses, with all other candidates treated as jointly tied at the lowest rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In both cases, aggregated rankings were derived using the same voting rule to ensure comparability. Specifically, we employed the Kemeny-Young algorithm to compute a consensus ranking from the individual evaluator preferences. Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Effect of Truncated Rankings in Peer Evaluation ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes the alignment between the resulting aggregated rankings and human judgments, measured by the Spearman correlation across evaluation queries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Pearson correlation with human rankings under complete and truncated peer evaluations on the GSM8K dataset.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complete Linear Ranking</span></th>
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Truncated Top-3 Ranking</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Mean</th>
<td id="A2.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.6949</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.6354</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">25%</th>
<td id="A2.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.6000</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5000</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Median</th>
<td id="A2.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.7714</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.7142</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T5.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">75%</th>
<td id="A2.T5.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.8857</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.8286</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results indicate that truncated rankings maintain a reasonable level of alignment with human preferences, though they consistently underperform complete rankings. Truncation appears to reduce the granularity of judgments and, by producing <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sparser preference structures</span>, leads to poorer overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Ranking Metrics and Aggregation Algorithms</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Kendall-Tau Distance and Coefficient</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Kendall-Tau distance between two permutations <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the number of discordant pairs:</p>
<table id="A3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(\pi,\sigma)=\sum_{i&lt;j}\mathbf{1}\left[\text{sign}(\pi(i)-\pi(j))\neq\text{sign}(\sigma(i)-\sigma(j))\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\pi,\sigma)=\sum_{i&lt;j}\mathbf{1}\left[\text{sign}(\pi(i)-\pi(j))\neq\text{sign}(\sigma(i)-\sigma(j))\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> returns <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Kendall-Tau <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">coefficient</span> is the normalized difference between concordant and discordant pairs:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\pi,\sigma)=\frac{C-D}{\binom{n}{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\pi,\sigma)=\frac{C-D}{\binom{n}{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are the number of concordant and discordant pairs, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example:</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[A,B,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[A,B,C]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=[B,A,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=[B,A,C]</annotation></semantics></math>, the only discordant pair is <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\frac{2-1}{3}=\frac{1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\frac{2-1}{3}=\frac{1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Kemeny-Young Rank Aggregation</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Kemeny-Young method finds a consensus ranking <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing the total Kendall-Tau distance to all <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> input rankings:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}=\mathop{\text{argmin}}_{\sigma}\sum_{k=1}^{m}D(\sigma,\pi_{k})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mtext>argmin</mtext><mi>σ</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}=\mathop{\text{argmin}}_{\sigma}\sum_{k=1}^{m}D(\sigma,\pi_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This problem is NP-hard, but efficient heuristics exist.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Procedure:</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pairwise Comparison:</span> Count how many times <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> ranks above <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> across all rankings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference Graph:</span> Construct a directed graph with weighted edges representing preference conflicts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minimum Feedback Arc Set:</span> Remove minimal-weight edges to make the graph acyclic; the topological sort gives the consensus.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example:</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given input rankings <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A,B,C],[B,A,C],[C,A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A,B,C],[B,A,C],[C,A,B]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to A</annotation></semantics></math> (1 disagreement), <math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\to C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\to C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The consensus ranking minimizing total discordance is <math id="A3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[B,A,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[B,A,C]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our implementation, we adapt this procedure by replacing <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\sigma,\pi_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\sigma,\pi_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\sigma,\pi_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\sigma,\pi_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> in the objective, enabling coefficient-based consensus aggregation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Dataset Statistics and Selection Criteria</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), we adopt a multi-level, multi-dimensional evaluation framework. We carefully select three representative benchmark datasets—GSM8K, MMLU, and GPQA—that span tasks of varying difficulty levels and reasoning demands, as summarized in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D Dataset Statistics and Selection Criteria ‣ LLMs Judge Themselves: A Game-Theoretic Framework for Human-Aligned Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These benchmarks are widely recognized for their effectiveness in probing high-level cognitive capabilities in LLMs, each emphasizing distinct dimensions of reasoning:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2024)</a></cite> focuses on basic mathematical reasoning. We sample 100 elementary-level arithmetic word problems to assess the model’s capabilities in computation, basic logic, and semantic understanding.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite> extends the evaluation to interdisciplinary knowledge integration. We select 114 multiple-choice questions across 57 subjects, spanning STEM fields (e.g., mathematics, physics) as well as humanities and social sciences (e.g., history, law), to evaluate the model’s cross-domain reasoning and conceptual abstraction skills.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al. (2023)</a></cite> is a high-level benchmark specifically introduced in this study. It comprises 50 open-ended generative questions that require comprehensive problem solving, often involving multimodal information fusion, multi-constraint optimization, and counterfactual reasoning. Models are required to produce full natural language solutions, posing greater demands on deep reasoning, creative thinking, and knowledge transfer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CEval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite> is a Chinese-language exam-style benchmark that evaluates model performance on national-level standardized test questions across subjects like law, medicine, and finance. We use a 100-example subset drawn from its development set, covering both humanities and STEM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IFEval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023a)</a></cite> is designed to evaluate instruction-following capabilities of LLMs. It includes a diverse mix of tasks involving goal specification, constraint following, and multi-step procedural reasoning. We sample 100 instances from various task types to cover general instruction comprehension.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MBPP</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021)</a></cite> targets program synthesis. Each item includes a short programming task with an English description and a set of unit tests. We sample 100 problems from the dataset to evaluate coding and functional reasoning abilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Creative Writing</span>. The Creative Writing benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite> consists of open-ended generation tasks such as story continuation, character development, and stylistic rewriting. We sample 100 prompts to assess models’ creativity, coherence, and narrative fluency under subjective evaluation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Key Properties of Evaluation Datasets</figcaption>
